  "name": "CHYLOMICRON QUALITATIVE BODY FLUID",
  "code": "1061",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"134\" data-end=\"421\">The <strong data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"170\">Chylomicron Qualitative Test</strong> in body fluid (typically <strong data-start=\"196\" data-end=\"207\">pleural</strong>, <strong data-start=\"209\" data-end=\"223\">peritoneal</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"228\" data-end=\"243\">pericardial</strong> fluid) detects the presence of <strong data-start=\"275\" data-end=\"291\">chylomicrons</strong>, which are large lipoprotein particles formed in the intestine to transport dietary triglycerides. The test is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"423\" data-end=\"687\">\n<li data-start=\"423\" data-end=\"535\">\n<p data-start=\"425\" data-end=\"535\"><strong data-start=\"425\" data-end=\"454\">Diagnose chylous effusion</strong>, a condition where lymphatic fluid rich in chylomicrons leaks into body cavities</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"536\" data-end=\"627\">\n<p data-start=\"538\" data-end=\"627\"><strong data-start=\"538\" data-end=\"572\">Differentiate chylous effusion</strong> from <strong data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"604\">pseudochylous effusion</strong> or other turbid fluids</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"628\" data-end=\"687\">\n<p data-start=\"630\" data-end=\"687\">Support evaluation of <strong data-start=\"652\" data-end=\"687\">lymphatic obstruction or trauma</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"652\" data-end=\"687\">Positive (Elevated) Result &ndash; Clinical Implications</strong></p>\n<p>Detection of chylomicrons in body fluid indicates a <strong data-start=\"809\" data-end=\"829\">chylous effusion</strong>, which may result from:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cause</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Thoracic duct injury</strong></td>\n<td>Trauma or surgical disruption leading to lymph leakage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malignancy (e.g., lymphoma)</strong></td>\n<td>Tumors obstructing the thoracic duct, especially lymphomas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Congenital lymphatic malformations</strong></td>\n<td>Seen in pediatric or genetic conditions affecting lymph drainage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Infections (e.g., tuberculosis)</strong></td>\n<td>Causing lymphatic obstruction or inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cirrhosis or nephrotic syndrome</strong></td>\n<td>Occasionally associated with chylous ascites</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
